DEEP[1]=('Chapter 18 is named Mokṣa-Sannyāsa Yoga, the yoga of freedom through renunciation. At 78 verses it is the longest chapter, and many teachers call it a summary of the whole Gītā. Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa as <i>Keśi-niṣūdana</i>, slayer of the horse-demon Keśī, a story from Kṛṣṇa’s youth in Vraja.',
 'Commentators note that the question goes back to the very first doubt of the Gītā. In chapter 1 Arjuna wanted to give up the fight; in 3.1 and 5.1 he asked whether renouncing action is better than doing it. Now, at the end, he asks for a final, careful distinction. Śaṅkara treats the chapter as gathering the teachings of the whole Gītā into one place. Rāmānuja says it sums up karma, jñāna and bhakti and then gives the final teaching of surrender.',
 'A good question asked at the end of a long study can bring everything together. Keep asking.')
DEEP[2]=('The tradition knows many kinds of <i>sannyāsa</i>: the formal monastic vow, with ochre robes and the giving up of the sacred fire, and the inner renunciation the Gītā has praised throughout (5.3, 6.1).',
 'Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa gives the views of the <i>kavayaḥ</i>, the wise, and the <i>vicakṣaṇāḥ</i>, the discerning, without yet giving his own. Śaṅkara says <i>kāmya karma</i>, actions done for desired results, such as rituals for wealth or heaven, are what sannyāsa gives up. Duties that must be done, <i>nitya karma</i>, are not included. Rāmānuja says the two terms ultimately mean the same thing: giving up attachment to fruits. Gandhi built his life on <i>tyāga</i> in this sense: full work, no personal claim.',
 'You may not be able to leave your work, but you can always loosen your grip on its rewards.')
DEEP[3]=('The Gītā’s time had lively debates between schools. The Mīmāṃsā school held that ritual action is the whole of dharma; some ascetic schools held that all action binds and should be dropped.',
 'Commentators admire the Gītā’s fairness: it states the opposing views before giving its own. Śaṅkara identifies the first view with followers of Sāṅkhya, who saw action as tainted because it always involves some harm. The second view belongs to the Mīmāṃsakas, for whom sacrifice, charity and austerity are always required. Rāmānuja agrees. The Gītā will take a middle road: keep the good works, drop the attachment.',
 'Hear both sides of a debate fairly before deciding. It makes your own view stronger.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 'Commentators note the phrase <i>niścayaṃ śṛṇu me</i>, “hear my conclusion”: the Lord now settles the debate of 18.3. The method is the one used in chapters 14 and 17: the guṇas. Even letting go can come from clarity, restlessness or dullness. Śaṅkara says the threefold division helps the seeker examine his own motive. Rāmānuja says only sāttvika relinquishment leads to freedom.',
 'Letting go can be wise, anxious or lazy. Before you give something up, ask which it is.')
DEEP[5]=('<i>Yajña, dāna, tapas</i>, sacrifice, charity and austerity, were the three pillars of religious life in the Gītā’s time. Chapter 17 described each in its three guṇa forms.',
 'Commentators note the strong claim: these purify <i>even the wise</i>, <i>manīṣiṇām</i>. Śaṅkara says they purify the minds of those not yet fully free. Rāmānuja says they are lifelong practices, part of worship. Many saints continued daily rites long after realisation, as an example and as an offering. Rāmānuja himself is remembered for keeping temple worship and daily study throughout his life.',
 'Don’t drop good habits as you grow. They keep purifying, and they help others too.')
DEEP[6]=('Kṛṣṇa calls this his <i>niścitaṃ matam uttamam</i>, “decided and highest view”: one of the few places where the Gītā states its own position in so many words.',
 'Commentators see this verse as karma yoga in its final form. Śaṅkara says even good works should be done without attachment, as a means to purify the mind. Rāmānuja says they should be done as worship of the Lord, without claiming the result. Madhva says they should be done as service to Viṣṇu. Tilak quoted this verse as proof that the Gītā teaches active life to the end. Vivekananda summarised it as “work for work’s sake”.',
 'Do good, and let go of what it brings you. That is the whole of karma yoga.')
DEEP[7]=('Arjuna’s wish in chapter 1 to drop his bow, born of grief and confusion, is a textbook case of this kind of relinquishment.',
 'Commentators note the full circle: the Gītā began with tāmasika tyāga and will end with Arjuna’s sāttvika resolve (18.73). Śaṅkara says giving up obligatory duty out of delusion is not renunciation but a fault. Rāmānuja says it comes from mistaking the body for the self. Many modern readers recognise the pattern in themselves: dropping important duties under the cover of high-sounding reasons, when the real cause is confusion or fear.',
 'Dropping duties in confusion is not renunciation; it is avoidance. Name it honestly.')
DEEP[8]=('',
 'Commentators say such giving up looks like renunciation but is really self-protection. Śaṅkara says it brings none of the fruit of true renunciation, which is knowledge. Rāmānuja says the person loses both the merit of the duty and the peace of letting go. Arjuna also feared the pain of killing his elders. Kṛṣṇa’s answer throughout has been that pain cannot be the measure of duty.',
 'Giving up because something is hard is not freedom. Check whether fear is making the decision.')
DEEP[9]=('<i>Kāryam ity eva</i>, “simply because it ought to be done”: the same phrase used of sāttvika worship in 17.11.',
 'Commentators call this the Gītā’s final answer to Arjuna’s first wish. Tyāga is not giving up action but giving up attachment to it. Śaṅkara says this purifies the mind and prepares for knowledge. Rāmānuja says it is the essence of karma yoga. Gandhi wrote that this verse resolves the riddle of how to act in the world without being bound by it.',
 'Keep doing what you must, and let go of what you crave from it.')
DEEP[10]=('',
 'Commentators note four marks of the wise relinquisher: filled with sattva, wise, doubts cut away, and even toward pleasant and unpleasant work. Śaṅkara says such a person is ready for knowledge. Rāmānuja says he does whatever pleases the Lord without preference. The ideal is practical. Cleaning, routine paperwork and difficult conversations are done with the same heart as the enjoyable parts of a job.',
 'Do the unpleasant task with the same heart as the pleasant one. It becomes lighter.')
DEEP[11]=('',
 'Commentators note that this repeats 3.5 at the end of the Gītā. Śaṅkara says complete giving up of action is possible only for the one who knows the Self and no longer identifies with the body. For all others, giving up the fruits is the practical path. Rāmānuja says the true tyāgī is the one who gives up the fruits. The verse quietly settles the debate: even renunciants must act; everyone can let go.',
 'You can’t stop acting, but you can always let go. Begin there.')
DEEP[12]=('The three fruits are traditionally explained as birth among gods (desired), birth among animals or in suffering (undesired), and human birth (mixed).',
 'Commentators note that even good results bind, by leading to more births to enjoy them. Śaṅkara says only the renunciant who knows the Self is free from all three. Rāmānuja says the one who gives up fruits to the Lord is free. The image of chains is common: an iron chain and a golden chain both bind. Freedom lies beyond the scoreboard of merit and demerit.',
 'Even good results can bind. Do good without keeping a ledger.')
DEEP[13]=('Verses 13–16 give a careful analysis of what makes any action happen, to loosen the ego’s claim to be the sole doer.',
 'Commentators debate the word <i>sāṅkhya</i>. Śaṅkara takes it as the Vedānta, which teaches knowledge of the Self. Rāmānuja takes it as the teaching of the Gītā itself. Others take it as the Sāṅkhya philosophy. Whatever its source, the analysis is practical. When we list all the causes of an outcome, the claim “I did it” shrinks, and so do pride and guilt.',
 'No action has a single cause. Seeing all the causes makes us humbler and calmer.')
DEEP[14]=('The five factors are <i>adhiṣṭhāna</i> (the body as seat), <i>kartṛ</i> (the doer, the ego), <i>karaṇa</i> (the instruments, the senses), <i>ceṣṭā</i> (the efforts) and <i>daiva</i> (the unseen factor).',
 '<i>Daiva</i> is read as providence, destiny, past karma, or the Lord’s will. Rāmānuja reads it as the Lord within, who is the final cause of all action. Śaṅkara reads it as the deities who govern the senses. A surgeon knows this well: the patient’s body, the surgeon’s skill, the instruments, the team’s effort and many unknown factors decide the outcome together.',
 'Do your part fully, and remember there are other factors. That brings both effort and humility.')
DEEP[15]=('Body, speech and mind are the three channels of action, the same triad used for austerity in 17.14–16.',
 'Commentators note that the five causes apply to all actions, good or bad. Śaṅkara stresses that the Self is not among them; it is the witness. Rāmānuja says the self is a doer, but its agency depends on the body, senses and the Lord. Madhva says the Lord is the chief doer. All agree that the ego’s claim to be the sole cause is wrong.',
 'Your deepest self is the witness of action, not its only engine. Let that lighten pride and guilt.')
DEEP[16]=('',
 'Commentators read this verse in two ways. Śaṅkara: one who thinks the pure Self acts does not see, because the Self never acts. Rāmānuja: one who thinks the self alone acts, without the body, senses and the Lord, does not see. Either way, the error is the same: the ego claiming everything. Many teachers note that this same error produces both arrogance in success and despair in failure.',
 'You are part of a large web. Don’t take all the credit, or all the blame.')
DEEP[17]=('This verse closely echoes 2.19 and 2.21, now at the end of the teaching.',
 'Commentators are careful with this verse. It states that a person free of ego, whose mind is untainted, is not bound even by killing. Śaṅkara says this applies only to one who knows the Self. Such a person cannot act from hatred or selfishness, so the verse cannot be used to justify harm. Rāmānuja says such a one acts only from duty, as the Lord’s instrument. The verse answers Arjuna’s fear of 1.36, that sin would cling to him. Gandhi read it as describing a state so pure that violence would not even arise.',
 'Actions done without ego, purely from duty, do not bind. Few reach that state; all can move toward it.')
DEEP[18]=('Verse 18 lists six elements: knowledge, the known and the knower (what prompts action); the instrument, the action and the agent (what makes it up).',
 'Commentators explain the sequence: first we know something, then desire or duty arises, then we act through instruments. Śaṅkara says knowledge, the object known and the knower together impel action. Rāmānuja says these elements, coloured by the guṇas, decide the quality of every act. The verse sets up the threefold analyses of 18.19–40, the Gītā’s fullest psychology of action.',
 'Understanding how action arises helps us act more wisely.')
DEEP[19]=('<i>Guṇa-saṅkhyāna</i>, “the science of the guṇas”, may refer to Sāṅkhya philosophy or simply to the teaching on guṇas in chapters 14 and 17.',
 'Commentators call verses 20–40 a complete map of the human being: how we know, how we act, who we are as doers, how we decide, how we persevere and what we enjoy, each in three qualities. Śaṅkara says the purpose is to help the seeker choose the sāttvika and give up the rest. Many modern readers find this section among the most practical in the Gītā for self-examination.',
 'Learn to see the quality behind your knowing and doing. Then you can choose to improve it.')
DEEP[20]=('Sāttvika knowledge sees <i>avibhaktaṃ vibhakteṣu</i>, the undivided in the divided.',
 'Commentators link this to 13.16 and 13.30. Śaṅkara says it is the knowledge of the one Self in all beings. Rāmānuja says it is seeing all selves as alike in nature, being pure knowledge. Madhva says it is seeing the one Lord in all. The verse is often quoted in discussions of unity in diversity, a phrase that became a motto of modern India.',
 'Look for the one thread running through the many beads around you.')
DEEP[21]=('Rājasika knowledge sees only separate entities: <i>nānā-bhāvān pṛthag-vidhān</i>, many things of different kinds.',
 'Commentators say this knowledge is practical and useful for daily life, but if taken as the whole truth it divides: my group and yours, my interests and yours. Śaṅkara says it sees a different self in every body. Rāmānuja says it sees selves as different in nature according to their bodies. Many conflicts in history have sprung from this kind of seeing.',
 'Differences are real, but they are not the whole story. Look also for what you share.')
DEEP[22]=('',
 'Commentators give the example of believing that the body is the whole self, or that one idol is the whole of God, without understanding. Śaṅkara says such knowledge is without reason and narrow. Rāmānuja says it mistakes a small thing for the whole. Today it might describe any fanaticism that cannot see beyond one fixed idea.',
 'Beware of narrow certainties held without reason. Keep the window open.')
DEEP[23]=('Sāttvika action is <i>niyata</i>, obligatory or right; <i>saṅga-rahita</i>, free of attachment; done without love or hatred, by one who does not seek the fruit.',
 'Commentators call this karma yoga in one verse. Śaṅkara says “without love or hatred” means without partiality. Rāmānuja says it is action done as worship. Many modern teachers apply it to professional ethics. A doctor treats every patient with the same care, a judge rules without favour, a teacher grades fairly.',
 'Act from duty and goodwill, not from craving or spite. That is the cleanest action.')
DEEP[24]=('',
 'Commentators note three marks: seeking desires, ego and strain. Śaṅkara says such action is done with the thought “I am the doer”. Rāmānuja says it binds by its very effort. Modern burnout often fits this verse: long hours driven by ambition or fear rather than purpose. The Gītā does not oppose hard work, but work that eats the worker.',
 'If your work is exhausting you, check the motive as well as the hours.')
DEEP[25]=('Tāmasika action ignores four things: <i>anubandha</i> (consequences), <i>kṣaya</i> (loss), <i>hiṃsā</i> (harm to others) and <i>pauruṣa</i> (one’s own capacity).',
 'Commentators find this a practical checklist. Before acting, consider the consequences, the cost, the harm to others and whether you are able. Śaṅkara says tāmasika action is begun in delusion. Rāmānuja says it brings ruin to oneself and others. The Kauravas’ decision to go to war, against every warning, is often cited.',
 'Before acting, ask four questions: consequences, cost, harm, capacity.')
DEEP[26]=('The sāttvika doer has <i>dhṛti</i>, resolve, and <i>utsāha</i>, enthusiasm, while being free of attachment and ego.',
 'Commentators note the balance: zeal and detachment together. The sāttvika doer is not dull or passive. Śaṅkara says he is unchanged in success and failure. Rāmānuja says he acts with joy for the Lord. Vivekananda often quoted <i>utsāha</i> as a key quality for workers. The ideal suits anyone: a farmer, a nurse, a craftsman, a leader.',
 'Bring enthusiasm and humility to your work, and stay steady whatever happens.')
DEEP[27]=('The rājasika doer is <i>harṣa-śokānvita</i>, swayed by joy and grief at every result.',
 'Commentators note the marks: passionate, eager for fruits, greedy, violent, impure. Śaṅkara explains <i>hiṃsātmaka</i> as one who harms others to get his ends. Rāmānuja says such a doer is bound by his own craving. The swinging between elation and despair is the clearest sign. Many find in this verse a description of the stress of modern competitive life.',
 'If every result sends you sharply up or down, rajas is in charge. Steady yourself.')
DEEP[28]=('',
 'Commentators list the eight marks: undisciplined, vulgar, stubborn, deceitful, malicious, lazy, despondent and procrastinating. Śaṅkara explains <i>naiṣkṛtika</i> as one who undermines others’ work. Rāmānuja says such a doer is moved by tamas. Many readers find the verse uncomfortably familiar. The remedy for tamas, as in 14.8, is gentle action: start small, start now.',
 'Procrastination is a mark of tamas. Start the smallest part of the task today.')
DEEP[29]=('',
 'Commentators note that both have three qualities. A person can have clear understanding but weak resolve, or strong resolve directed to the wrong ends. Śaṅkara says buddhi is the faculty of determination and dhṛti the power of steadiness. Rāmānuja says both must be sāttvika for the path to be walked. Arjuna lacked both in chapter 1; he will have both in 18.73.',
 'How you decide and how you persevere both matter. Strengthen both.')
DEEP[30]=('Sāttvika understanding knows <i>pravṛtti</i> and <i>nivṛtti</i>, when to act and when to withdraw, and what to fear and not fear.',
 'Commentators note that this is exactly what the demonic nature lacked (16.7). Śaṅkara says sāttvika understanding knows bondage and freedom. Rāmānuja says it knows the path of action and the path of renunciation. The ability to tell when to act and when to stop is perhaps the most practical wisdom of all.',
 'Clarity about when to act, and when to stop, is a treasure. Cultivate it.')
DEEP[31]=('Rājasika understanding knows dharma and adharma, but <i>ayathāvat</i>, not as they really are.',
 'Commentators say desire bends the mind, so it sees right and wrong unclearly. Śaṅkara says such understanding mixes things up. Rāmānuja says it sees duty clouded by self-interest. Many difficult moral decisions fall into this trap: we half-know the right thing but our wants distort it.',
 'When desire is strong, double-check your judgement with someone you trust.')
DEEP[32]=('Tāmasika understanding is <i>viparīta</i>, inverted: it takes wrong for right and sees everything upside down.',
 'Commentators note that this is worse than confusion: it is certainty about the wrong things. Śaṅkara says darkness covers the mind so that it sees all things reversed. Rāmānuja says it mistakes adharma for dharma. History is full of terrible acts done with complete conviction. The verse warns that feeling sure is not the same as being right.',
 'Sometimes we are most certain when we are most wrong. Stay humble.')
DEEP[33]=('Sāttvika <i>dhṛti</i> holds the mind, breath and senses steady through yoga, <i>avyabhicāriṇī</i>, without wavering.',
 'Commentators note that true fortitude is directed inward. Śaṅkara says it is the steadiness that keeps mind, breath and senses on the path to liberation. Rāmānuja says it is resolve fixed on the Lord. The connection to chapter 6 is clear: meditation needs this kind of steady will (6.25).',
 'Real strength is the steadiness to keep your mind on course. Practise it in small ways.')
DEEP[34]=('Rājasika resolve pursues <i>dharma, kāma, artha</i>, duty, pleasure and wealth, three of the four aims of life (<i>puruṣārtha</i>), with attachment.',
 'Commentators note that the fourth aim, <i>mokṣa</i>, freedom, is missing. Śaṅkara says such resolve holds on to these aims for their fruits. Rāmānuja says it is resolve bound by desire. The three aims are not wrong; the Hindu tradition honours them. But pursued without the fourth, they keep one on the wheel.',
 'Pursue life’s aims wisely, and keep the fourth in view: freedom.')
DEEP[35]=('Tāmasika resolve clings to five things: sleep, fear, grief, despondency and arrogance.',
 'Commentators find this verse sharp: some people show great determination in holding on to their misery. Śaṅkara calls it the steadiness of a fool. Rāmānuja says it is resolve in the service of tamas. Modern psychology describes similar patterns of stubborn rumination and learned helplessness.',
 'Don’t be determined to keep your fears. Be determined to let them go.')
DEEP[36]=('Everyone seeks happiness, <i>sukha</i>. The Gītā asks what kind, and what it costs.',
 'Commentators note the phrase <i>abhyāsād ramate</i>, “one rejoices through practice”: sāttvika happiness grows with practice, unlike pleasures that fade with repetition. Śaṅkara says this happiness ends sorrow. Rāmānuja says it comes from the self’s nearness to the Lord. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad’s choice between <i>śreyas</i>, the good, and <i>preyas</i>, the pleasant, is close to this teaching.',
 'Ask which kind of happiness you are chasing, and what it will cost later.')
DEEP[37]=('Sāttvika happiness is <i>viṣam iva agre</i>, like poison at first, and <i>amṛtopamam</i>, like nectar in the end.',
 'Commentators give examples: study, exercise, meditation, kindness to difficult people. Each is bitter at first and sweet later. Śaṅkara says the bitterness comes from the effort of discipline. Rāmānuja says the sweetness comes from the clarity of the self. Parents and teachers have used this verse for generations to encourage children through hard work.',
 'Good habits taste bitter at first. Persist; the sweetness comes.')
DEEP[38]=('Rājasika happiness is <i>amṛtopamam</i>, like nectar at first, and <i>viṣam iva</i>, like poison in the end.',
 'This is the mirror of 18.37. Commentators give examples: overeating, gossip, gambling, anger released. Each is sweet in the moment and bitter after. Śaṅkara says it arises from the contact of senses with objects. Rāmānuja says it binds one to craving. The verse is often paired with 5.22: pleasures born of contact are wombs of sorrow.',
 'Before a tempting pleasure, imagine how it will taste an hour later.')
DEEP[39]=('Tāmasika happiness arises from <i>nidrā, ālasya, pramāda</i>: sleep, laziness and negligence.',
 'Commentators note that this “happiness” deludes from beginning to end. Śaṅkara says it covers the mind throughout. Rāmānuja says it is the pleasure of dullness. Modern readers recognise it in numbing habits: endless scrolling, too much sleep, drifting through the day. It is not rest; real rest refreshes.',
 'Numbing out is not rest. Choose rest that leaves you clearer, not foggier.')
DEEP[40]=('This verse closes the analysis of the guṇas that began in 18.19.',
 'Commentators note that no being in nature, not even the gods, is free of the guṇas. Only the Self, beyond nature, is untouched. Śaṅkara says this is why one must go beyond the guṇas by knowledge. Rāmānuja says it is by devotion to the Lord (14.26). The verse is a call to patience: everyone, including ourselves, is a mixture.',
 'Don’t expect anyone, including yourself, to be pure sattva. Be patient, and keep moving up.')
